ut when I pass a file argument, the code path changes entirely. The `listPaths` function doesn't apply the dummy/zero-block filter that `listAll` does—it just calls `entryForPath` for each path, which does a stat and finds the mount, then calls `Statfs` on it. So `gdf /dev/null` and `gdf -a /dev/null` would return identical results since the file argument bypasses the filtering logic.\n\nThat means I can't use a file argument to test the `-a` flag's behavior. The flag only matters when listing all filesystems with no arguments, but that's where I hit the edge case issue with entries appearing and disappearing between calls.\n\nI think the best move is to just implement the code and run the tests. If the no-argument `-a` test fails due to the edge case, I'll handle it then with a normalizer or by skipping that specific test.\n\nFor the `-l` flag, I'm going to use the `MNT_LOCAL` flag from the filesystem stats on macOS to determine if a filesystem is local or network-based, which is cleaner than trying to match against a hardcoded list of filesystem types.\n\nNow I'm implementing the `printLine` function using a strings.Builder to format each column with proper spacing and alignment...\n\nActually, I need to reconsider the SIGPIPE handling here. Using a single `fmt.Println` call with the built string is cleaner than multiple writes, though Go's SIGPIPE handling operates at the signal level rather than per-write, so either approach would work correctly.\n\nI'll go with building the parts into a slice and joining them with spaces, then printing once. This keeps the output clean and efficient.\n\nNow I'm ready to start writing the actual code. But first, I need to think through the imports carefully — I'm using `format.PadLeft` and `format.PadRight` from the format package, so I'll keep that import. The existing code has its own `humanSize` function rather than using `format.HumanSize`, and since the design decision says not to rewrite code that already satisfies the req